{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q023_homeodomain_heterogeneous_fold","answer":"What remains justified: homology. In SCOPe 2.08 both d1p7jd_ (sunid 94286, 1p7j chain D) and d1ztra1 (sunid 125655, 1ztr A:0-59) sit under the same species node (sunid 46692, Drosophila melanogaster) of the same protein entry, Engrailed Homeodomain (sunid 46691), in family a.4.1.1 Homeodomain (sunid 46690), superfamily a.4.1 Homeodomain-like (sunid 46689), fold a.4 DNA/RNA-binding 3-helical bundle (sunid 46688), class a. The lowest shared node is the family, and in fact the protein/species level: these are two point mutants of the same ~60-residue Engrailed homeodomain sequence (1p7j is the K52E crystal structure at 2.1 A; 1ztr is the NMR solution structure of the L16A mutant; 1ztrA aligns to its cluster representative 1p7iA at 93% identity over 59 columns). Shared family in SCOPe implies common ancestry, and here that is backed by near-identity of sequence, so homology (and the inference that the wild-type protein is a sequence-specific DNA-binding homeodomain) is firmly justified and is not undermined by the hf flag. Family-level placement also legitimately supports the superfamily and class assignments.

What the hf annotation changes: 'hf' (heterogeneous fold) is a domain-scoped, non-inherited inconsistency flag on d1ztra1 meaning the domain adopts a different fold than the family exemplar while having similar sequence and number of secondary-structure elements. SCOPe kept it in a.4.1.1 because classification follows evolutionary relationship (sequence identity to Engrailed homeodomain), not because its 3D structure matches the three-helix-bundle fold. The 1ztr entry itself is described as a 'denatured state / folding intermediate' structure of the L16A mutant: the helices are present but their tertiary packing is not the native homeodomain arrangement. Consistent with this, SCOPe stores no FATCAT structure hits for 1ztrA (its cluster representative is 1p7iA, so even that absence is only indirect), and its quality metrics are poor (SPACI/AEROSPACI 0.0024, NMR, effective resolution 4, strongly negative WHAT_CHECK packing, Ramachandran, chi-angle and backbone scores) versus 1p7j (SPACI 0.44, 2.1 A X-ray, positive WHAT_CHECK scores). The L16A substitution is also a core-destabilizing mutation, and residue 16 lies in the hydrophobic core, so the structure is a deliberately perturbed, non-native state, not an alternative functional conformation.

Why they are not interchangeable templates or training examples: (1) Template use. d1p7jd_ (or any non-hf a.4.1.1 member) is a valid template for modelling homeodomain tertiary structure, helix packing, or DNA-recognition geometry (helix 3 in the major groove, N-terminal arm in the minor groove). d1ztra1 is not: using it as a comparative-modelling template or superposition reference would transfer a non-native helix arrangement to the target even though sequence identity is ~93%. Sequence identity here is a reliable guide to homology but a misleading guide to coordinate transfer. (2) Function transfer. Function (DNA binding) is inferred from homology to the family and from wild-type/K52E-type native structures, not from the 1ztr coordinates; one should not infer a DNA-binding mode, or a loss of it, from the 1ztr model, and one should not assume the K52E mutant (which alters a DNA-contacting residue) reproduces wild-type affinity either. (3) Training/benchmark data. For fold-recognition, structure-prediction, or fold-level classification training, including d1ztra1 labelled simply 'a.4, three-helical bundle' injects a coordinate set that contradicts its label; similarly, treating the two as redundant duplicates (e.g. collapsing them in a 40% or 95% identity ASTRAL subset and keeping whichever survives) can silently leave a non-native structure as the sole representative. Filter on the hf (and related inconsistency) flags, prefer high-AEROSPACI representatives such as 1p7j/1p7i, and keep d1ztra1 only for tasks where a folding-intermediate ensemble is the intended object. (4) Representative selection. SCOPe classification of d1ztra1 is correct evolutionarily but its lineage should not be read as a statement that its coordinates exhibit the a.4 fold; any analysis that equates family membership with structural equivalence must check annotations, not just the SCCS. Caveats: the hf flag is release-specific curator judgment (2.08); absence of stored FATCAT hits is not proof of structural dissimilarity; and confirming the degree of non-native packing requires inspecting the coordinates/ensemble or the primary NMR paper, not the SCOPe record alone.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__compare_scop_domains","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry","mcp__scope__get_scop_chain_hits","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1p7jd_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1p7jd_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1p7jd_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1p7jd_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ztra1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ztra1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ztra1/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ztra1/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ztr","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ztr/chains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ztr/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ztr/quality","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1p7j","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1p7j/chains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1p7j/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1p7j/quality","/api/v1/releases/2.08/chains/1ztrA/structure-hits?limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/46691","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/46691/annotations","pretrained knowledge: 1ztr is the Religa et al. 2005 NMR structure of the Engrailed homeodomain L16A folding intermediate; 1p7j is the K52E mutant crystal structure"]}]}
